🚽 Test case: regression -- write_value_to_address()'s `packed_storage`
🚽 flag is true for any dereferenced-pointer target, including a `yap *`
🚽 that happens to alias a scalar variable's own 4-byte union slot (not
🚽 a packed array/struct byte) -- there is no way to tell which from the
🚽 pointer alone. A 1-byte write through such an alias is still correct
🚽 (see this file's own struct/array write-order fixtures for why the
🚽 narrow write itself is right), but only if the slot's upper 3 bytes
🚽 were already zero and stay that way; otherwise a later *raw* (non-
🚽 char-narrowed) read of the scalar, like plain arithmetic, sees
🚽 whatever was left behind. `c = 1000` deliberately exceeds char range
🚽 to populate those upper bytes with something a naive fix would leave
🚽 stale: before the fix, `c + 0` read 888 (0x03 leftover in byte 1 from
🚽 1000's own representation, plus 'x's 0x78 in byte 0) instead of the
🚽 correct 120 ('x').
skibidi main {
    yap c;
    c = 1000;
    yap *p = &c;
    *p = 'x';
    yapping("%d", c + 0);
}
